{"title":"2021 Fall AMC 12A Problems/Problem 11","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1_(Pythagorean_Theorem)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1 (Pythagorean Theorem)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Power_of_a_Point)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Power of a Point)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Coordinate_Geometry)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Coordinate Geometry)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_TheBeautyofMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by TheBeautyofMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_(Logic_and_Geometry)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution (Logic and Geometry)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_Fall_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Consider two concentric circles of radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/6/b7616a230c77f0d9a577e5bca01aa06f1c35c457.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$17$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/b/aeb45c8359d7e646a579268e0a2d40569dddb229.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$19.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"25\" height=\"14\" /> The larger circle has a chord, half of which lies inside the smaller circle. What is the length of the chord in the larger circle?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/5/0056f3452ffd00b7d7efeac9e975cac49d7155c7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 12\\sqrt{2} \\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ 10\\sqrt{3} \\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ \\sqrt{17 \\cdot 19} \\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ 18 \\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ 8\\sqrt{6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"507\" height=\"23\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_1_.28Pythagorean_Theorem.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1_(Pythagorean_Theorem)\">Solution 1 (Pythagorean Theorem)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_Fall_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1 (Pythagorean Theorem)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Label the center of both circles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. Label the chord in the larger circle as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/9/f89e0ca688f912718b42d2324528b037f44fda43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{ABCD}$\" width=\"58\" height=\"15\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> are on the larger circle and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> are on the smaller circle. Construct the radius perpendicular to the chord and label their intersection as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" />. Because a radius that is perpendicular to a chord bisects the chord, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> is the midpoint of the chord. \n</p><p>Construct segments <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/1/c51eee107f2b552caca08ea4f3cd1078a32b22ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AO}$\" width=\"28\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/2/5d29ab3cedcc24c862f97e5d7f2bb1da18b7067e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{BO}$\" width=\"29\" height=\"15\" />. These are radii with lengths 17 and 19 respectively. \n</p><p>Then, use the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Pythagorean_Theorem\" title=\"Pythagorean Theorem\">Pythagorean Theorem</a>. In <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/d/47d8720cc891188cc83f7032ea5cba0f1fd31a19.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle OMA$\" width=\"63\" height=\"13\" />, we have\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/2/e62c2a71bed386c39dc1e39d04848ea7b7f423cf.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} OM^2 &amp; = OA^2 - AM^2 \\\\ &amp; = OA^2 - \\left( \\frac{AD}{2} \\right)^2 \\\\ &amp; = 19^2 - \\frac{AD^2}{4} . \\end{align*}\" width=\"188\" height=\"120\" />\n</p><p>In <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/6/f56c6b549f1e7be150552138f1562aa03ded184b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle OMB$\" width=\"64\" height=\"13\" />, we have\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/f/abf100cdc2f01bf08521f9cf0b6968b48bc9cc71.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} OM^2 &amp; = OB^2 - BM^2 \\\\ &amp; = OB^2 - \\left( \\frac{BC}{2} \\right)^2 \\\\ &amp; = OB^2 - \\left( \\frac{AD}{4} \\right)^2 \\\\ &amp; = 17^2 - \\frac{AD^2}{16} . \\end{align*}\" width=\"189\" height=\"172\" />\n</p><p>Equating these two expressions, we get \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/3/883a41feee0742cf6eaf6cfce372db8953c08704.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[19^2 - \\frac{AD^2}{4} = 17^2 - \\frac{AD^2}{16}\\]\" width=\"198\" height=\"39\" />\nand <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/9/7190f789ec8fd7ce12949a8c47d9e2cfba7b048e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD=\\boxed{\\textbf{(E) }8 \\sqrt{6}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"134\" height=\"35\" />.\n</p><p>~eisthefifthletter and <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.professorchenedu.com\">Steven Chen</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Power_of_a_Point.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Power_of_a_Point)\">Solution 2 (Power of a Point)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_Fall_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Power of a Point)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Draw the diameter perpendicular to the chord. Notice that by symmetry this diameter bisects the chord.\n</p><p>Call the intersection between that diameter and the chord <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />. In the smaller circle, let the shorter piece of the diameter cut by the chord be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />, making the longer piece <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/7/6c7bbe78d8af59a76875c72073fd5f1a530bc7bc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$34-x.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"58\" height=\"15\" /> In that same circle, let the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> be the length of the portion of the chord that is cut by the diameter. \n</p><p>The radius of the larger circle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> more than the radius of the small circle. So, in the larger circle, the shorter piece of the diameter cut by the chord is of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/8/eb8ab0ec5027c055e1b34dc345b1d8532b611bce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> and the longer piece is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/8/b58f8518b7713f1705c544f52952a6e2d8ff8308.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$36-x.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"58\" height=\"15\" /> As given in the problem, the bisected length of the chord in the larger circle is twice as much, so it is of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/c/26cab3f0704de3232f601b03d02a45d46103c172.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" />. By <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Power_of_a_Point_Theorem\" title=\"Power of a Point Theorem\">Power of a Point</a>, we can construct a system of equations\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/4/d645f4d098ac0f94ccc9a8802f7a1e8044f6b374.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x(34-x) = y^2\\]\" width=\"115\" height=\"20\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/8/1388b8820407af666a2201e44629797b410e977e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(x+2)(36-x) = (2y)^2.\\]\" width=\"187\" height=\"20\" />\nExpanding both equations, we get\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/c/92c87ac4a0e8ea763ddaac0fd6a1cbbfd1c5a666.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[34x-x^2 = y^2\\]\" width=\"108\" height=\"19\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/8/3c864349b27647436e157bdf5c402a77fff88b8b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[36x-x^2+72-2x = 4y^2,\\]\" width=\"204\" height=\"19\" />\nin which the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/3/d6348a908cdd54de5583078afbad62d9e6f5a026.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$34x$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"28\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/6/466763d8093c6aab3836e9df67ceffcc6a2d4936.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-x^2$\" width=\"30\" height=\"15\" /> terms magically cancel when we subtract the first equation from the second equation. Thus, now we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/a/3ba2c0f21eb5bdd9f497d6c05af74b2b50c65404.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[72 = 3y^2 \\implies y = 2\\sqrt{6} \\implies 4y = \\boxed{\\textbf{(E)} \\: 8\\sqrt{6}}.\\]\" width=\"356\" height=\"33\" />\n</p><p>-fidgetboss_4000\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Coordinate_Geometry.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Coordinate_Geometry)\">Solution 3 (Coordinate Geometry)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_Fall_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Coordinate Geometry)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Represent the circles as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/e/dbed69498f9454d0c4ae71116f7cd9326758d9be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^{2}+y^{2}=17^{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"109\" height=\"20\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/9/b493fd44f0ec2ea5606977bd96dcaa057f064c76.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^{2}+y^{2}=19^{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"109\" height=\"20\" />. Solving for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> in these equations we obtain <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/e/93e9b3b648f7c55ff07a1dcf464e2c8ca591cdbd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\sqrt{17^{2}-y^{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"119\" height=\"24\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/2/06262c487b1f5ebba8cdbc04b63b9c26c026543e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\sqrt{19^{2}-y^{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"119\" height=\"24\" />. Because half of the chord is in the smaller circle, the larger circle should have an <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> value that is twice as big as the smaller circle's <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> value. We set up the equation:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/6/4a67438ba4ddb2c9acb971882669dc6770d10464.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2\\sqrt{289-y^{2}}=\\sqrt{361-y^{2}}\\]\" width=\"202\" height=\"21\" /> \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/8/e587065fb256696be661915a4d07a0e2cc31488e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[4(289-y^{2})=361-y^{2}\\]\" width=\"178\" height=\"20\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/5/a8586ddfee6d8532cd90d75b3c8c8e5d1023594d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[y^{2}=265\\]\" width=\"67\" height=\"19\" />\nSubstituting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/c/15c99786e47b27e98eb5c555b6a7489d1138ad0e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y^{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"15\" height=\"18\" /> into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/9/b493fd44f0ec2ea5606977bd96dcaa057f064c76.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^{2}+y^{2}=19^{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"109\" height=\"20\" />, we obtain <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/3/793fed9f042e6ff3b21e907c7fd6f6e1c10b1670.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\sqrt{96}=4\\sqrt{6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"127\" height=\"21\" />. However, this is only half of the chord length, so we must double it to obtain <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/d/edd9b6b3d375ce1dab4e282d53817ec51fcf5014.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(E)} \\: 8\\sqrt{6}}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"83\" height=\"35\" />\n</p><p>-helpmebro\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_TheBeautyofMath\">Video Solution by TheBeautyofMath</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_Fall_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by TheBeautyofMath\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/ToiOlqWz3LY\">https://youtu.be/ToiOlqWz3LY</a>\n</p><p>~IceMatrix\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_.28Logic_and_Geometry.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_(Logic_and_Geometry)\">Video Solution (Logic and Geometry)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_Fall_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution (Logic and Geometry)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/iG1vVXeTv58\">https://youtu.be/iG1vVXeTv58</a>\n</p><p>~Education, the Study of Everything\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_Fall_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"2\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_Fall_AMC_12A\" title=\"2021 Fall AMC 12A\">2021 Fall AMC 12A</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_Fall_AMC_12A_Problems\" title=\"2021 Fall AMC 12A Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_Fall_AMC_12A_Answer_Key\" title=\"2021 Fall AMC 12A Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2021_Fall_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"2021 Fall AMC 12A Problems/Problem 10\">Problem 10</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=